module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.LogIdealAlgebraizationData

/-!
# LogIdealImageStrictEtale

A step of logarithmic image-ideal algebraization.
(blueprint: lem-trf-log-ideal-image-strict-etale).
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
universe u
open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite
namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Strict étale maps preserve fine coherent ideals and restrict their structural
image ideals, including the canonical ideal inclusion. Source: ILO, Exposé VIII
3.3.4(ii), 3.3.7 and 3.4.2; Stacks, Tags 03DV and 0GN7
(blueprint: lem-trf-log-ideal-image-strict-etale).

Proof: For an étale base map, the relative small-étale site is the slice over
that map. Strictness identifies the monoid sheaf on every object of this slice
with the original monoid sheaf, compatibly with restrictions and alpha.
Pull a fine chart back to this slice. Its finitely generated integral monoid
is unchanged, so these charts show that the source log structure is fine.
Pull back finite nonempty chart generators of I. The definition of pullback
ideal says stalkwise that its elements are products of an image of an I-section
with a log element. Since the original ideal is generated by E, these germs
are precisely multiples of the pulled-back E. This proves the ideal axioms
and the nonempty fine coherent ideal condition on the source.
On any further source étale object V, strictness and the slice equivalence
identify the geometric stalks and their structural maps. The ideals generated
by alpha of the two log ideals coincide: products contribute multiples of the
pulled-back generators, and those generators occur in the pulled-back ideal.
The definition by geometric-stalk membership therefore gives the displayed
equality of sets of functions, also on nonaffine V. The canonical tensor
composition comparison identifies the pullback of the original ideal inclusion
with its restriction on the slice. Flatness of the étale base map and the
ideal tensor-pullback comparison identify this inclusion with that of K.comap.
Consequently K.comap algebraizes the source structural image. All equalities
are of subsheaves of functions, so the inclusion is fixed. This argument also
works when the source is empty, when an image generator is zero, or when the
image ideal is the whole structure sheaf. -/
theorem logIdeal_image_strict_etale {X Y : Scheme.{u}} (M : EtaleLogStructure X)
    (hM : M.IsFine) (I : M.IdealFamily) (hI : M.IsNonemptyFineCoherentIdeal I)
    (K : X.IdealSheafData) (hK : M.IsImageIdealAlgebraization I K)
    (N : EtaleLogStructure Y) (f : EtaleLogMap N M) (hf : f.IsStrictEtale) :
    N.IsFine ∧ N.IsNonemptyFineCoherentIdeal (f.pullbackIdeal I) ∧
      N.IsImageIdealAlgebraization (f.pullbackIdeal I) (K.comap f.base) ∧
      ∀ (he : Etale f.base) (V : Y.Etale),
        N.imageIdealSections (f.pullbackIdeal I) V =
          M.imageIdealSections I
            (@Scheme.Etale.mk X V.left (V.hom ≫ f.base) (by
              have := he
              infer_instance)) := by sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
